{"file":{"content":"I do now have a much sharper description of what a counterexample would have to look like:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{it must be a high-complexity, mixed-adically coherent, calibrated ghost that resists every finite max-plus certificate.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is a very narrow class.\n\n## 1. What has now been eliminated\n\nYour repetition-rigidity theorem is a serious new filter. It kills the most natural symbolic counterexample class.\n\nEarlier, the plausible counterexample profile was:\n\n[\nb_n\\in{1,2},\\qquad\n\\Pr(b=1)=2-\\log_2 3,\n]\n\nwith Sturmian/Beatty-like balanced discrepancy. That was the obvious calibrated low-letter ghost. Now it is dead.\n\nMore generally, in a banded no-descent window\n\n[\nx_n\\in [Y,WY],\n]\n\nonce\n\n[\n2^{p+1}>Y(W-1),\n]\n\ntwo equal length-(p) valuation blocks force equal points, hence an actual integer cycle. Combined with the Baker cycle bound, long banded no-descent windows above height (Y) must have almost full factor diversity at scale\n\n[\np\\approx \\log_2 Y.\n]\n\nSo a counterexample can no longer be Sturmian, morphic, linearly recurrent, polynomial-complexity, or any bounded-discrepancy low-complexity word. It must be much more random-looking.\n\nThat is the main consequence:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{a counterexample must imitate randomness in factor complexity while still carrying non-coboundary shadow bias.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is an uncomfortable combination.\n\n## 2. My best current guess for a counterexample profile\n\nIf Collatz is false, I would now expect a counterexample to look like this.\n\nIt is not periodic. It is not low-complexity. It is not generated by a clean rotation/Sturmian rule. It has valuation sequence (b_n) satisfying:\n\n[\n\\frac1N\\sum_{n<N} b_n\\to \\log_2 3\n]\n\nor at least stays close enough to avoid C1 descent, while still maintaining a persistent finite non-coboundary phase bias:\n\n[\n\\frac1N\\sum_{n<N}\\chi'(2)^{-b_n}\n\\not\\to\n\\mathbb E_{\\rm geom}[\\chi'(2)^{-b}].\n]\n\nInside every long banded window at height (Y), for\n\n[\np\\sim \\log_2 Y,\n]\n\nit must have nearly maximal length-(p) factor diversity:\n\n[\nP(p)\\gtrsim M-p+1.\n]\n\nSo it must look locally like a de Bruijn/random word at the exact scale where (2)-adic repetition would force a cycle.\n\nBut it cannot be genuinely annealed-random, because annealed-random behavior is exactly what the finite spectral theorem kills. It has to be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{random-looking in block diversity, but phase-biased in a finite non-coboundary character.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is my current best description.\n\nIf I had to search symbolically, I would search for **critical high-complexity Markov/subshift words** with:\n\n1. mean (E[b]\\approx \\log_2 3);\n2. persistent non-coboundary shadow bias;\n3. no repeated length-(p) blocks in banded windows;\n4. coherent realizability in the mixed (2^a3^b)-tower;\n5. no finite max-plus descent certificate.\n\nI would not search among Sturmian words anymore. They are excluded.\n\n## 3. The frontier is now an (S)-unit/subspace theorem problem\n\nYour phrase “many coherent block identities” is now exactly the right next object.\n\nFor a block of length (p) beginning at (n), one has\n\n[\nx_{n+p}\n=======\n\n\\frac{3^p x_n+A_{n,p}}{2^{B_{n,p}}},\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\nA_{n,p}\n=======\n\n\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}3^{p-1-j}2^{B_{n,j}}.\n]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n[\n2^{B_{n,p}}x_{n+p}\n------------------\n\n# 3^p x_n\n\nA_{n,p}.\n]\n\nThe right-hand side is a structured sum of (S)-units:\n\n[\nA_{n,p}\n=======\n\n\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}\n3^{p-1-j}2^{B_{n,j}}.\n]\n\nIn a long banded window\n\n[\nx_n\\in[Y,WY],\n]\n\nthe (x_n)'s are ordinary integers in a short multiplicative interval, while the (A_{n,p})'s are sums of (2)-(3) (S)-units determined by distinct valuation blocks.\n\nThe intended Subspace-Theorem-style principle would be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{too many coherent relations }\n\t2^{B_i}X_i-3^pY_i=A_i\n\t\\text{ with }X_i,Y_i\\in[Y,WY]\n\t\\text{ force the }S\\text{-unit exponent vectors into finitely many linear patterns.}\n}\n]\n\nThose linear patterns should correspond to repeated or eventually periodic valuation blocks. But repetition rigidity/Baker cycle exclusion already kills those.\n\nThis is the next frontier.\n\n## 4. A concrete theorem to aim for\n\nI would formulate the next target as follows.\n\n**Banded (S)-unit rigidity theorem.**\nFix (W>1). There exist constants (A,\\kappa>0) such that for any no-descent orbit segment\n\n[\nx_n,\\dots,x_{n+M}\\subset [Y,WY],\n]\n\nwith (Y) large and\n\n[\np=\\lfloor \\log_2 Y\\rfloor,\n]\n\neither:\n\n1. the segment contains a repeated length-(p) valuation block, hence an integer cycle by repetition rigidity; or\n2. the number of realizable distinct length-(p) block identities is at most\n[\n\\exp((1-\\kappa)p);\n]\nor\n3. a max-plus descent certificate appears at some mixed level (2^a3^b).\n\nYour complexity-height theorem says a counterexample must force case 2 to fail, i.e. must have many distinct blocks. The desired (S)-unit theorem would then force case 1 or 3.\n\nThis is the right kind of theorem, but it is not currently a standard theorem you can quote.\n\n## 5. What to compute next: the high-complexity trap search\n\nNow that low complexity is dead, the computational search should reverse direction.\n\nSearch for windows that maximize all four properties simultaneously:\n\n[\n\\text{critical mean},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{non-coboundary shadow bias},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{high factor complexity at }p\\sim \\log_2 Y,\n]\n\n[\n\\text{mixed-tower certificate resistance}.\n]\n\nFor each candidate window, compute the block identities:\n\n[\n2^{B_{i,p}}x_{i+p}-3^px_i=A_{i,p}.\n]\n\nThen build the exponent vectors\n\n[\n\\mathcal E_{i,j}\n================\n\n(B_{i,j},,p-1-j)\n]\n\nappearing in\n\n[\nA_{i,p}\n=======\n\n\\sum_j 2^{B_{i,j}}3^{p-1-j}.\n]\n\nLook for linear dependencies or concentration of these exponent vectors. The Subspace-Theorem route predicts that certificate-resistant high-complexity windows should nevertheless show algebraic structure in these exponent vectors. If they do not, then the next theorem must be more probabilistic/entropy-based.\n\nA useful diagnostic:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{rank}*{\\mathbb Z}\n\\langle\n\\mathcal E*{i,j}-\\mathcal E_{i',j'}\n\\rangle.\n]\n\nCompare high-deviation windows to ordinary windows. If the rank collapses in high-deviation no-descent windows, the (S)-unit route has traction.\n\n## 6. Search for counterexamples in the right space\n\nA brute-force integer search is not the right search now. Search in symbolic/mixed-adic space.\n\nGenerate candidate words (w) of length (M) satisfying:\n\n[\n\\left|\\sum_{n<M}(b_n-\\log_2 3)\\right|\\le C,\n]\n\n[\n\\left|\\frac1M\\sum_{n<M}\\chi'(2)^{-b_n}-G_\\chi\\right|\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\n[\nP_w(p)\\approx M-p+1\n\\quad\\text{for }p\\sim\\log_2 Y,\n]\n\nand no obvious finite max-plus certificate.\n\nThen compute the (2)-adic realization class\n\n[\nx\\equiv x_w\\pmod{2^{B_M}},\n]\n\ntake least positive representatives, and test:\n\n1. Does the realized prefix stay positive and no-descent?\n2. Does it enter a banded window?\n3. Does it lift coherently modulo (3^r)?\n4. Does a mixed-tower certificate appear?\n\nThis is the meaningful counterexample search.\n\nIf every high-complexity phase-biased candidate either fails positive realization or gets a certificate, you will have data for the joint-tower rigidity theorem.\n\n## 7. The “no long banded windows” alternative\n\nYour current frontier has a second escape route:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{the orbit forever avoids long banded windows.}\n}\n]\n\nThis should also be attacked.\n\nIf an orbit has no long banded windows, then it crosses multiplicative bands quickly. A no-descent divergent orbit must repeatedly move from\n\n[\nY\n]\n\nto\n\n[\nWY\n]\n\nwith average drift\n\n[\n\\sum(\\log_2 3-b_n)>0.\n]\n\nThat means a high density of (b=1)'s over growth phases. But (b=1) coherence is exactly where the (2)-adic tower starts forcing negative ghosts such as (x=-1).\n\nSo formulate a **band-crossing certificate theorem**:\n\nIf an orbit crosses ([Y,WY]) too quickly without descent, then its (b=1)-density is large enough to trigger a mixed (2)-adic max-plus certificate, unless it is approaching the negative (2)-adic ghost.\n\nThis would handle the “forever avoids long bands” case.\n\nThus the final dichotomy becomes:\n\n[\n\\text{long banded windows}\n\\Rightarrow\nS\\text{-unit/block complexity rigidity};\n]\n\n[\n\\text{no long banded windows}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{band-crossing }2\\text{-adic certificate}.\n]\n\nThat is a good final program.\n\n## 8. The most plausible counterexample, after all filters\n\nAfter your latest theorem, I would revise my guess.\n\nA counterexample, if it exists, is no longer Sturmian-like. It would be closer to a **critical pseudorandom subshift**:\n\n* alphabet mostly ({1,2}), with occasional (3+) corrections;\n* mean calibrated near (\\log_2 3);\n* high factor complexity at scale (p\\sim\\log Y);\n* persistent non-coboundary character bias;\n* no repeated (p)-blocks inside any long banded window;\n* mixed-adically coherent through all (2^a3^b);\n* all periodic approximants killed by Baker.\n\nThat is very close to a “designed pseudorandom counterexample.” It is hard to imagine it arising from one integer orbit, but this is the remaining logical possibility.\n\nSo my current best guess remains:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{there is no positive counterexample.}\n}\n]\n\nBut the strongest surviving counterexample model is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{a high-complexity calibrated pseudorandom valuation word with finite non-coboundary bias.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is the object to search for.\n\n## 9. The next proof stack\n\nI would now organize the next phase into three named targets.\n\n### Target 1: Banded (S)-unit rigidity\n\nProve that high-complexity banded no-descent windows force an (S)-unit/subspace structure, hence repeated/effectively periodic blocks or a max-plus certificate.\n\n### Target 2: Band-crossing certificate\n\nProve that avoiding long banded windows forces enough (b=1)-density to trigger a (2)-adic certificate.\n\n### Target 3: Mixed-tower certificate enumeration\n\nImplement finite verification over (2^a3^b) for traps extracted from symbolic candidates, with Karp max-cycle checks and potential extraction.\n\nThese three cover the remaining frontier.\n\n## 10. Bottom line\n\nYour new repetition-rigidity theorem has killed the low-complexity ghost class. What remains is much harder but much narrower:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{high-complexity, calibrated, phase-biased, mixed-adically coherent traps.}\n}\n]\n\nThe next mathematics should be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tS\\text{-unit/subspace rigidity for many block identities}\n}\n]\n\nplus\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t2\\text{-adic band-crossing certificates}\n}\n]\n\nfor the no-banded-window escape.\n\nThe next computation should search not for integers directly, but for symbolic candidates satisfying all surviving constraints and then test their mixed-tower realizability and max-plus certificates.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_next_steps.md","numLines":435,"startLine":1,"totalLines":435},"type":"text"}